course: dictionary
lesson: 1189
title: Bearing the brunt
level: primary-6
stars: 0
explain: The word brunt is almost always heard in phrases like «bear the brunt of» and «take the brunt of». On its own it is rare, so learn it as a chunk of words.
explain: Brunt means the worst part, or the main force, of something bad, such as an attack, a storm or blame. Look at the words around it to find out what the bad thing is and who gets hit hardest.
explain: Otter says, «the Mole and the Water Rat bore the brunt of the fighting». The clue is fighting, so the two of them suffered most. Bore here means suffered, just like took in «took the brunt».
explain: Check your meaning by swapping it in. «The Mole and the Water Rat took the hardest part of the fighting» still makes sense, so you have understood it.
fact: Brunt means the worst part or main force of something bad.
fact: To bear, or take, the brunt of something means to suffer the most from it.
fact: «The coast took the brunt of the storm.» means the coast suffered the most from the storm.
fact: «Served in the ranks» means was an ordinary soldier, not a leader.
exercise: «The coast took the brunt of the storm.» What does brunt mean here? | The calm, quiet edge of the sea | *The worst force of the storm | A boat that sailed away | A small gift from the weather
exercise: In «the Mole and the Water Rat bore the brunt of the fighting», who suffered the most? | *The Mole and the Water Rat | Badger | Otter | Nobody at all
exercise: Find and copy the phrase that means suffered the most. | *bore the brunt | did little | served in the ranks | the fighting
test: «The village bore the brunt of the flood.» What does this tell us? | The village built a bridge over the flood | *The village suffered the most from the flood | The village was far away and safe from the flood | The village held a party after the flood
test: In Otter's words, «Badger was the mastermind», so who bore the brunt of the fighting? | Nobody | Badger | Otter | *The Mole and the Water Rat
exam: What does «served in the ranks» mean in Otter's words? | A bridge over the river | A cook who made the soup for everyone | A king who ruled the land | *An ordinary soldier, not a leader
